Chapter: IntPow

 The `^` operator on integers.

 It had no arm in the emitter's binary-operator dispatch and fell through to
 the catch-all, which returns `reg-rax` having emitted nothing. So `^`
 answered whatever was in that register: `2 ^ 3` gave 1130508, a code
 address, and did so for every program this compiler has ever built. The
 `__ipow` helper was emitted into every binary and never called once.

 Nothing in the tree used the operator, which is why no test and no battery
 could have noticed. It is documented in the operator table in
 `docs/DevelopersGuide.md` all the same.

 The exponents here cover the helper's three paths. A zero exponent answers
 1; a negative exponent answers 0, which is deliberate rather than
 accidental -- `emit-ipow` patches that branch to its own `li rax, 0` and
 returns, which is the truncation of a reciprocal toward zero and agrees
 with what integer division does; and the square-and-multiply loop handles
 the rest. The odd and even exponents matter separately because the loop
 only multiplies the accumulator on a set low bit.

 The negative-exponent answer is not stated in `docs/DevelopersGuide.md`'s
 operator table. It is pinned here as the behaviour the implementation
 deliberately has, not as a semantic this test is choosing.

Section: Ops

  ipow : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ipow (a) (b) = a ^ b

Section: Report

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
    print-line-uni ("two-cubed " & show (ipow 2 3))
    print-line-uni ("two-fourth " & show (ipow 2 4))
    print-line-uni ("three-fifth " & show (ipow 3 5))
    print-line-uni ("ten-third " & show (ipow 10 3))
    print-line-uni ("any-zero " & show (ipow 7 0))
    print-line-uni ("any-one " & show (ipow 7 1))
    print-line-uni ("one-big " & show (ipow 1 62))
    print-line-uni ("neg-base-even " & show (ipow (0 - 3) 4))
    print-line-uni ("neg-base-odd " & show (ipow (0 - 3) 3))
    print-line-uni ("neg-exponent " & show (ipow 5 (0 - 2)))
    print-line-uni ("zero-base " & show (ipow 0 5))
  end
